// PARAM: --analysis congruence
// min(3, 7) is 3 and max(3, 7) is 7, both fixed by the source.
//
// The concrete mechanism is congruence_min and congruence_max
// (Congruence_Special.thy), which are both the join. A residue class
// carries no order information -- congruence_lt is the constant None -- so
// the domain cannot tell which operand is smaller, and the only sound
// answer that is always available is a class containing both. Here that is
// 3 (mod 4) for each, which congruence_eqb declines on because it is not a
// singleton. Returning the join is not a shortcut: it is forced whenever
// the operands are distinct classes.
//
// The two operands agreeing is the one case that stays exact, since the
// join of a class with itself is that class. --analysis interval and
// --analysis sign both decide this program (../precision/01, ../precision/
// 02) from order information Congruence does not have.
void main() {
  x := 3;
  y := 7;
  z := min(x, y);
  w := max(x, y);
  __voblint_check(z == 3); // UNKNOWN
  __voblint_check(w == 7); // UNKNOWN
  v := min(x, x);
  __voblint_check(v == 3) // PROVED
}
